--- body ---
## Goal

Every review phase ends within its time budget with a verdict, or with a
recorded "no report". This bounds the worst case and is directly checkable.
Reducing the variability of review time is the main lever; whether the
startup wait is a fixed cost or variability is settled by measurement.

## Why

Raids 1080 and 1089 overran the author's 20-minute closing window: 79 and 59
minutes from first review to merge (PRs 1389, 1390). The time went to seats
that failed to start, stalled or were dropped, not to reviewing itself.
Unbroken reviews are already fast (JETP-observer #154: 1.8 min; #152:
10.6 min). Evidence and counts: docs/discussions/2026-10-14-1096-design.md
("What the evidence says about the 20-minute window") and its seat records.

## Sequencing

Blocked by 1144 (the fold of `review-pr` into `gaze`, child 3 of tracker 1100), the real prerequisite:
budgets are set on the chain that survives, not on the layers 1100 and 1176 remove. The tracker 1100
itself is not the blocker. Design ticket 1096 is closed.

## Rescoped 2026-10-21 (author: "rescope 1099")

Since the ticket was written, these items were done or made moot, with the reason:
- Startup failures on PRs 1389 and 1390 (Claude CLI argument error, native seat thread limit, read-only
  file system): fixed by earlier work; not seen in the 2026-10-21 runs.
- The isolation guard dropping seats: not seen on 2026-10-21; the guard-misfire rule (prefer deleting)
  applies if it returns.
- Test and validation waiting: `make check` fell from 353 s to 76 s (PR 1583, worktree-gc readlink);
  local checks run in parallel (PRs 1534, 1548); the full suite runs once, on the merge candidate, in
  the local gate (tracker 1045); forge CI no longer runs (Actions disabled on 2026-10-21). The author's
  extension of 2026-10-15 (total development feedback latency) is therefore delivered by those tickets;
  its phases are not repeated here.
- Seat-selection machinery (width, depth, scoping, floor): deleted by ticket 1176; the orchestrator
  chooses seats and depth.

What stays, three items:
1. **Measure the seats.** The review result states, per seat that ran, its wall time (or `timed out`,
   `failed to start`, `skipped`), including failed starts. This is the same list ticket 1155 adds
   (role, family, model id as observed); do it once there. One raid's closing time is broken down by
   phase from these lines.
2. **Cap the external reader.** The cross-family read run through `codex exec` has no cap in the skill; on
   2026-10-21 it ran 25 minutes twice without output. A hard cap, set from the measured times, and a
   `no report (timed out)` line in the result; the caller continues without it and says so.
3. **Never drop a seat silently.** A planned seat that returns nothing is named in the result as
   `no report`; a retry-once and the degraded rule are already in the review skill (PR 1557).

## Exit criteria

- [ ] The review result lists, per seat, its wall time or its failure (shared with ticket 1155).
- [ ] The external reader has a hard cap and records `no report (timed out)`; a test or a stubbed run
      shows the caller continuing.
- [ ] The raid measurement is handed off: delivered by child 1184 after this lands (one raid broken
      down by phase from the recorded lines, its closing time reported against the author's 20-minute
      window, no new numerical budget beyond the reader's cap). This PR files 1184; it cannot measure
      a raid that runs on code not yet merged.
- [ ] No seat is dropped silently: a missing seat appears as `no report`.
- [ ] Nothing here requires a particular agent runtime.
